description:
This project is a game, built on Minecraft, focused on a type of game called Skyblock. It has a lot of qualities of an RPG but overall would be classified as a Skyblock server.

The server turned into a profitable business that has supported me through university, and allowed me to get an apartment in Toronto.

While I cannot share any code as the server is not open-source, I have written this detailed outline with code snippets. You can view gameplay on Youtube by searching "Midnightsky MC", here is one for convenience: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ruial5IPnh8.

As you can see, I've spent many many hours working on this.
![Screenshot 2026-07-18 at 11.02.05 PM.png](/design-assets/images/5637016.webp)


## 1.1 What is MidnightSky?

A horizontally-scalable Minecraft network where:

- Dozens of **Game** servers all act as one.
- A microservice-based **MasterControl** server orchestrates dozens of Game servers
- A lightweight **Proxy** plugin (BungeeCord) manages authentication & routing of players.  
- All communication uses a custom, Netty-based binary RPC layer (KNet) with code-generated stubs.

## 1.2 Network Architecture

![Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 3.04.19 PM.png](/design-assets/images/3263580.webp)

**Why so many custom libraries**

Throughout MidnightSky there are a few things that are non-standard custom libraries, with KNet being the most notable. If I was to remake MidnightSky perhaps I would use more standard libraries like Spring Boot, PL4J, or gRPC, but I found that it was an invaluable learning experience to write things myself and learn how everything works.

The KNet project was designed specifically for MidnightSky and provides an extremely simple-to-use interface. All that is needed is to write one interface defining the API methods, add one annotation, implement the interface and boom, ready to use. Binary RPC was chosen over REST APIs for performance as HTTP in comparison is quite slow for the real-time constraints MidnightSky admits.

## 1.2 Features

I've built hundreds of features for MidnightSky over the years. The vast majority of features are concentrated in both the Adventure and Island systems, which are mentioned here first in this high-level list of features.
- Durable Island System with fast load/save times and dozens of sub-features like island bots, mining nodes, custom farming, custom fishing mechanics and animations, sophisticated loot mechanics, various island upgrades, expansion islands, automation primitives, etc.
- Multiple Adventure Maps complete with bosses, 6 tiers of enemies, POIs, loot mechanics, a levelling system, enemy ability and animation system, etc.
- Social systems such as Auction House, Direct Messaging, Public Island warps, Chat systems for displaying your loot and inventory, and Alliances (which has many systems of its own like taxes on members).
- Periodic Games and Events like Crates, Black market auction, Contests, Jackpot (Lottery), Alliance Wars (a complex bracketed game of its own), King of the Hill, and Last Man Standing.
- Secure trading systems like /trade (command), chance-based trading with /coinflip, and combat-based trading and wagering with /duel.
- Over 500 unique items and loot drops each with its own role and functionality.
- Onboarding systems like /challenges and /help.
- Dozens of administrative and moderation tools including a network log system featuring 30 different types of player logs, dozens more system logs, and AI chat moderation.
- Complex Stat and Effect system with over 100 different types of effects and 12 different stats that affect combat and more.
- A punishment system
- 20 Different Player Leaderboards in areas like Most Enemies Killed, etc.
- Server configuration system similar to Git, complete with configuration commits, change history, rollbacks, and instant cross-server config hot-reloads when changes are pushed.
- Levelcap – a Progressional and Economic Limiter/Barrier to keep all players within a certain progressional range at each point during a season. This also doubles as a scheduling mechanism for updating certain configs on certain dates during the season.
- 350+ unique in-game GUIs to facilitate easy use of almost all features.
- 550+ unique in-game commands to facilitate quick usage of all features.
- Custom developed Command and GUI frameworks for each of the above (MidnightCommands and FluidGUI)

## 1.3 Coroutine Usage
I utilize coroutines extensively in this project, I've absolutely fallen in love with them. The most useful applications of them in this project include the following

**TickDispatcher**  
The tick dispatcher is a coroutine dispatcher on Game Servers that runs coroutines on the main Server Thread. Spigot (server plugin framework) has it's own scheduler that allows you to schedule tasks, but this *greatly* simplifies scheduling tasks. For example, in an animation, using scheduling I would need to write code centered around a tick counter and have different parts execute depending on the "time" in ticks since the animation began. Code would look like this:
```java
    public void update(Player target) {

        if (ended) return;

        if (phase == 0) {
            // ...
            for (int i = 0, standListSize = standList.size(); i < standListSize; i++) {
                EntityRef<ArmorStand> ref = standList.get(i);
                ArmorStand stand = ref.get();
                if (stand != null) {

                    Location loc = location.clone().add(Math.cos(i * rotationAmount) * currRadius, relY, Math.sin(i * rotationAmount) * currRadius);
                    loc.setYaw((float) (relY * 180));

                    setEntityLocation(stand, loc.getX(), loc.getY(), loc.getZ(), loc.getYaw(), loc.getPitch());
                }
            }
        } else if (phase == 1) {
            int phaseTick = tick - phase0Ticks;

            // ...

            Location destination = target.getLocation().add(0.5, 0, 0.5);

            for (int i = 0, standListSize = standList.size(); i < standListSize; i++) {
                EntityRef<ArmorStand> ref = standList.get(i);
                ArmorStand stand = ref.get();
                if (stand == null) continue;

                // ...

                setEntityLocation(stand, toTeleport.getX(), toTeleport.getY(), toTeleport.getZ(), toTeleport.getYaw(), toTeleport.getPitch());

                if (percent >= 1) {
                    stand.remove();
                    target.damage(0.01);
                }
            }
        }

        if (++tick >= animTicks) {
            stop();
        }

        if (tick >= phase0Ticks) {
            phase = 1;
        }
    }
```

But with coroutines, I can write code that looks much cleaner:

```kotlin
suspend fun play(config: AirdropConfig, location: Location) {
        fall(location)
        spawnChest(config, location)
        val backBlocks = explosion(location)

        delayTicks(30 * 20)

        location.block.fakeBreak()
        location.block.type = Material.AIR

        backBlocks.forEach { (offset, type) ->
            location.block.getRelative(offset.x, offset.y, offset.z).type = type
        }
    }
```

Notice the delayTicks() function which suspends the coroutine for 30 seconds before proceeding, but without blocking the main Server Thread. Also notice that fall() can be suspending and implement its own completely separate animation that can be chained onto the beginning of this larger Airdrop animation.


**Replacement for CompletableFuture**
Many Java developers will recognize "Callback Hell", and in the past, many parts of MidnightSky fell victim to it. Coroutines allow me to replace 50 chained completable future calls with simple suspending function calls that are as easy to read as synchronous code. I don't actually have an example of what it looked like before, because I have rewritten all instances of it to use coroutines, but here is an image from google that is similar.

![Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 7.11.53 PM.png](/design-assets/images/4096215.webp)

---

## 4. Custom Libraries

### 4.1 KNet
Collaboration with Magnus Petz (https://github.com/MrMineO5)

1. **API Definition**  
Create an interface that will outline each of the procedure calls in the common module. In this interface, you will define one suspending function for each api call you want to add to the protocol. You must also add the @ProxiedInterface annotation that will generate supporting code. One example is shown below, function descriptions have been removed for brevity.
```kotlin
@ProxiedInterface
interface MasterIslandService {

    suspend fun register()
    suspend fun listIslands(): Set<SUUID>
    suspend fun listRegistrations(): Map<String, List<SUUID>>
    suspend fun save(island: UUID, save: Pair<SJsonMeta, ByteArray?>, sessionId: Long? = null): Boolean
    suspend fun deleteIsland(island: UUID)
    suspend fun triggerUnload(island: UUID): Boolean
    suspend fun sendToIsland(checkAccess: Boolean, player: UUID, island: UUID, location: IslandLocation?)
    suspend fun moveIsland(island: UUID, server: String?)
    suspend fun leaderboard(rank: Int, num: Int): List<IslandLeaderboardEntry>
    // ... Omitted for brevity
}
```

2. **Implementation and Registration**  
Once you have implemented your interface in the MasterControl project, you must register it by making this call:
```kotlin
registerNewProxy(MasterIslandManager)
```

3. **Usage**  
Now, you can use this service from the Spigot project like this:
```kotlin
val proxy = getProxy<MasterIslandService>()
proxy.sendToIsland(!force, playerId, target.id, null)
```
Just make sure to handle any exceptions that might be thrown as uncaught exceptions thrown on the side of MasterControl are propagated to the caller on the Spigot server (or vice versa if MasterControl is the caller).

4. **Limitations**  
Only primitives, classes marked with ```@Serializable``` (Kotlinx), and ```GravSerializable``` (Legacy System) classes can be used as parameter and return types in KNet APIs. However, some utility classes are fine by default such as ```UUID```.

5. **Implementation**
Below is a sketch of the implementation architecture of KNet
![Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 3.57.11 PM.png](/design-assets/images/4892357.webp)

### 4.2 MidnightCommands

There are over 500 commands on MidnightSky and so I made a library that makes it very easy to create new commands. Here's an example of what a command class looks like:

**Command:** /duel accept {id}

*this command is never actually used by players but is used as a callback for a clickable chat message that says "Click to Accept"*
```kotlin
object CmdDuelAccept : NewMidnightCommand("accept") {
    init {
        noPermission()
        params {
            integer("id")
        }

        executor {
            if (isServerFlag(ServerFlag.RESTRICTED_ITEM_ACCESS)) cmdError("You cannot do that here.")

            val id: Int = arg("id")
            val duel = getProxy<MasterDuelService>().get(id) ?: return@executor cmdError("Duel not found")
            GuiDuelAccept(duel).open(player)
        }
    }
}
```

As you can see, you need to extend NewMidnightCommand("alias") and pass in the main alias for the command in the constructor. In the init block, you build the command using a standard kotlin builder DSL. By default a command needs a certain permission to use, but you can disable that by calling noPermission(), then you can configure your parameters in the params {} block, and then finally define the executor, which executes the command on the main thread (by default) in a coroutine.

Here's one more example for you:

**Command:** /top reset {board}

*this is an administrative command used to reset a leaderboard*
```kotlin
object CmdTopReset : NewMidnightCommand("reset") {
    init {
        permission("midnight.top.reset")
        params {
            options("leaderboard") {
                getter { TopLeaderboardType.entries }
            }
        }

        executor {
            val service = getProxy<MasterLeaderboardService>()
            service.reset(arg("leaderboard"))
            respond("<a>Leaderboard reset.".toComp())
        }
    }
}
```

### 4.3 KAsyncWorld
Library for extremely fast world-editing and world-manipulation.
Please see https://mattlack.com/project/1748545289816

### 4.4 KSerializer
This is just an expansion to Kotlinx Serialization that adds an encoding format that has a few features we need like binary types, nullability support, and importantly speed. the KJson encoding which KSerializer provides is much much faster than the default Json encoder that kotlinx provides out of the box. It also supports null unlike protobuf.

### 4.5 KComp
Minecraft now uses a certain component-based chat system, and making chat messages is a little more complicated than writing a string now. Strings, however, have great support in kotlin for interpolation, and we wanted that for Chat Components, so this project is a compiler plugin that allows safe component interpolation within strings. It's probably better if I just show an example. Consider this line of code:
```kotlin
ChatHandler.instance.send("<c>Profiler: <f>$clickMe".toComp(), visibility)
```
```clickMe``` is a Component instance, so normally, this would call toString() on it and not at all give what I want, but KComp will instead insert the component into the larger message's component along with all colours, click events, hover events, etc.

### 4.6 FluidGUI
Like commands, MidnightSky has lots and lots of GUIs, over 300 of them and they are a pain in the butt to make using regular Spigot APIs. So, meet FluidGUI, a library allowing me to declaratively define GUIs and not have to worry about placing individual buttons in individual slots. The main time-saver of this library is the fact that it works like an html flex box – you just define the general layout and the gui places the components for you. Example:

```kotlin
class GuiExpansionBuffEditor(val config: ExpansionBuffConfig, val back: FluidGui) : FluidGui("Buff Editor".toComp(), 5) {
    init {

        background = fillerItem(Material.BLACK_STAINED_GLASS_PANE)

        container(width = 9, height = 1) {
            background = fillerItem(Material.BLACK_STAINED_GLASS_PANE)
            addComponent(backButton(back))
            flexLayout(justify = FlexLayout.Justify.CENTER)
        }

        field {
            label("<b>Growth Rate Boost".toComp())
            description("Percentage boost in crop growth rate")
            get { (config.growthBoost * 100.0).toString() }
            set { config.growthBoost = it.toDouble() / 100.0 }
        }

        field {
            label("<b>Spawn Rate Boost".toComp())
            description("Percentage boost in mob spawn rate")
            get { (config.spawnBoost * 100.0).toString() }
            set { config.spawnBoost = it.toDouble() / 100.0 }
        }

         // ...

        container(width = 9, height = 1) {
            dynamic {
                for (effect in config.effects) {
                    button {
                        renderer {
                            // ...
                        }
                        onClick {
                            if (it.isShiftClick) {
                                config.effects -= effect
                                update()
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }

                button {
                    renderer {
                        MidnightItemBuilder(Material.LIME_STAINED_GLASS_PANE)
                            .name("<a>Add Effect")
                            .addLore("<7>Click to add a new effect.".toComp())
                            .build()
                    }

                    onClick {
                        coroutineMain {
                            // ...
                        }
                    }
                }

            }

            flexLayout()
        }

        flexLayout(justify = FlexLayout.Justify.SPACE_EVENLY, align = FlexLayout.Justify.SPACE_EVENLY)
    }
}
```

