## 5 MasterControl

### 5.1 Architecture Overview
![Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 3.23.58 PM.png](/design-assets/images/9959726.webp)
MasterControl employs a microservice architecture with a selection of internal services that support the network services.

### 5.2 Services
Each master service is given a single-threaded Coroutine Scope for its tasks so that there isn't much headache worrying about thread-safety as each service is single-threaded and completely owns the resources it uses, furthermore context-switching is predictable and can only occur at suspension points. Inter-service calls are thread-safe as they switch to the thread of the called service for the call.

Services have database access through the MasterDatabase internal service and each service can define their own Database access interfaces, and a MongoDB implementation for clean separation.

Services can list individual Game servers (Spigot) by way of the internal ServerManager service, and make RPC calls to them.

On-shutdown, RPC calls are paused, and each service is allowed time to complete running calls before their coroutine scope is closed.


## 7. Core Gameplay Systems – Deep Dives

### 7.1 Islands

### 7.1.1 Island Loading
The island system is facilitated by MasterIslandService, which orchestrates and synchronizes the loading and saving of islands on different game servers. Once an Island Server starts up, it calls register() in the island service to notify MasterControl that it is ready to start serving players. A player will teleport to their island by running the ```/is go``` command. This command will call sendToIsland() on the island service which will then perform the following sequence of actions:
- Retrieve the island header and check island access permissions if applicable
- Find out if the requested island is already loaded and teleport the player there if so
- If not, pick the island server with the least current load\*
- Fetch the island data and world from the database
- Generate a session ID for the island \*\*
- Command the picked server to load the island, providing the data and world to it
- Once loaded, update data structures that keep track of island load states and locations
- Teleport the player to the island

\* It actually picks a server using a probability distribution weighted by the inverse of each server's current load, this prevents a situation (which actually happened in production) where hundreds of islands could pick the same low-load server at the same time, instantly overloading it.

\*\* Session IDs track each period of an island being loaded, if by some nightmare the same island is ever loaded on two different servers, then on the next save event (every minute) only the most recent session's data will be persisted and the out-of-date instance will be unloaded. Thankfully, this has never happened with this Island System (though it did happen with the previous system).

Parts this procedure are island-wise mutually exclusive, such as the island loading and data structure updating. This ensures consistency even under heavy load. Mutual Exclusivity is facilitated by the UUIDMutex class, which ensures that two coroutines with the same key (UUID) cannot enter certain critical sections at the same time by suspending all but one of the coroutines until the "holder" finishes. This is a custom class that had to be made because a normal lock would block the service's single thread.

On the Game Server's side, loading an island looks like this, after receiving the data (IslandData) and the world data (data structure containing PackedWorld instances from the KAsyncWorld project).

- Start timings (times each part of this)
- Figure out how many worlds the island has, decode each one
- Create Island Instance
- Create Island Worlds
- Set island status to FULL indicating it is fully loaded (as opposed to data-only, no worlds)
- Enable the island (starts island mechanics and loads data structures from IslandData)
- Record the island in a data structure
- Push the island header to the Island Service

### 7.1.2 Island Unloading
Once the last player leaves an island, the Island Server will wait a few seconds, and then issue a request to the Island Service to unload the now-empty island. Then, the IslandService issues the unload request *back* to the Island Server.

(This is to ensure a consistent order of execution, in particular it ensures that MasterIslandService always knows that an island is unloading *before* unloading actually begins, avoiding various race conditions. Generally I have found that having a uni-directional flow of control makes everything better)

The Island Server then unloads the island and sends the saved data back to the service for processing and persistence.

### 7.1.3 Data
Island Data culminates in the ```IslandData``` class which is supplied to the ```Island``` instance during loading. The Island instance has a number of modules for mechanics that either work directly on the IslandData object, or load into specialized data structures and then save back to it later. The aim is for most of the Island mechanics code to function **on** the IslandData, through trial and error this has proven to be the most organized way of structuring the many mechanics classes that Islands need.

### 7.1.4 Worlds
Islands can have multiple worlds, but have one main world. Each world is a KAsyncWorld in-memory world, which is desirable due to the volatility of these worlds. It would be inconvenient and slow to need to clean up file directories each time an island is loaded or unloaded. Worlds have extra data defining **Island Structures**, each of which has data, a handler instance (one per type), dimensions and a position. Examples of these structures include:

- Island Portals
- Deadzone portals
- Spawners
- Mining Nodes
- Permission Blocks
- Power Adapters
- Dimensional Chests
- Conduit
- Deadzone Workbench

There is a class called ```IslandEnvironment``` which holds pretty much all the extra information you would need about an IslandWorld that is separate from its block content. To be specific, it contains:
```kotlin
    val collectionChestManager = IslandCollectionChestManager(this)
    val botManager = IslandBotManager(this)
    val realmPortalTracker = IslandRealmPortalTracker(this)
    val structures = IslandStructureTracker(this)
    val naturalGrowthTracker: IslandNaturalGrowthTracker = IslandNaturalGrowthTracker(this)
    val mobHandler = IslandMobHandler(this)
    val fishTracker = FishTracker(this)
```

There are also many **EnvironmentHandler** classes, all singleton, that handle all the world mechanics of every island on the server. Here is an example, a lot is omitted as these handlers do a lot of... pizazz (animation):
```kotlin
class IslandFishingHandler : EnvironmentHandler() {
    @EventSubscription
    private fun onFish(ev: PlayerFishEvent) {
        val hand = ev.player.inventory.itemInMainHand.toMidnight() ?: run {
            // ...
            return
        }
        // ... Much omitted

        if (ev.state == PlayerFishEvent.State.CAUGHT_FISH) {

            val loc = ev.hook.location

            val islandWorld = ev.player.serverPlayer.currentIslandWorld ?: return

            val sp = ev.player.serverPlayer

            val availableTypes = // ...

            if (availableTypes.isEmpty()) return

            val fishLuck = ev.player.serverPlayer.getEffects(Effects.FISH_LUCK)
                .sumOf { it.arguments[0].toDouble() }

            // ... Much is omitted

            val rodXP = FishingProgression.settings[type]!!.rodXP

            val fishingEvent = //...

            Bukkit.getPluginManager().callEvent(fishingEvent)

            if (fishingEvent.isCancelled) return

            // ... Much is omitted

            // Launch fish
            val fishTracker = islandWorld.environment.fishTracker

            val tempGroup = FishGroup(FishArea(), 20 * 6)
            fishTracker.addGroup(tempGroup)

