that it still uses an old serialization system and so can't be sent over KNet, so needs to be changed to a List of ByteArrays. I'd like to migrate this to Kotlinx Serialization at some point when I get time.

### 7.3 Stats and Effects

I designed a unified **Stat & Effect** framework to power combat modifiers, loot chances, mob behaviors, and a host of gameplay mechanics. Unlike one-off event listeners, this system lets me declare Effects in a consistent Kotlin DSL with typed parameters, semantic stacking rules, and event-driven triggers.

**Defining Effects**  
Effects subclass `Effect`, specify an identifier, human-readable name, description, and typed parameters:

```kotlin
class EffectPvpDefense : Effect("pvp_defense") {
    init {
        description("Reduces incoming PvP damage by a percentage.")
        params {
            double("percent") {
                description("Damage reduction percentage")
                default(0.0)
                tags(EffectParamTag.ADDITIVE)
            }
        }
    }

    @EventSubscription
    private fun onDamage(event: StattedDamageEvent) {
        val player = event.victim as? ServerPlayer ?: return
        val reduction = player.getEffects(this).sumOf { it.arg<Double>("percent") }
        if (reduction <= 0.0) return
        event.multiplyBase(event.damageStat, -reduction / 100.0)
        procMessage(player)
    }
}
```

**Parameters & Stacking**  
Typed parameters (`integer`, `double`, `boolean`, `string`, etc.) are declared in a `params` block. Defaults, descriptions, and semantic tags (`EffectParamTag.ADDITIVE`, `EffectParamTag.MULTIPLICATIVE`, etc.) drive stacking logic and help generate in-game tooltips.

**Event-Driven Triggers**  
The `@EventSubscription` annotation hooks effects into any Spigot or custom event. Inside those handlers, I query active Effects on a player or entity (`getEffects(this)`), aggregate parameters, and apply custom logic (damage modification, healing, loot manipulation, etc.).

**Centralized Registration**  
All effects register themselves in `Effects.kt`, making it trivial to browse the full catalog:

```kotlin
object Effects {
    val PVP_DEFENSE        = EffectPvpDefense().register()
    val ADV_MOB_EFFICIENCY = EffectAdvMobEfficiency().register()
    // ...100+ other effects...
}
```

**Legacy Migration**  
This DSL replaces our older `AbstractEffectHandler` API that relied on manual event wiring and untyped argument arrays. The new approach eliminates boilerplate, enforces type-safety, and standardizes behavior across a hundred distinct effects.

This unified framework has drastically reduced boilerplate, improved readability, and made balancing game mechanics both faster and safer.

### 7.4 Stat Effects & Effect Providers

While core attributes such as Health, Damage, Strength, and more all follow the same Effect‑based paradigm (via the `StatEffect` helper and `StatEffectHandler`), their values are ultimately sourced from a collection of modular **EffectProviders**. Stats themselves are registered alongside other Effects in `Effects.kt`:

```kotlin
// Stats are implemented as StatEffects with an operation and amount.
val STAT_STRENGTH     = StatEffect("stat_strength",         "Strength").register()
val STAT_ATTACK_SPEED = StatEffect("stat_attack_speed",     "Attack Speed").register()
val STAT_DAMAGE       = StatEffect("stat_damage",           "Damage").register()
val STAT_HEALTH       = StatEffect("stat_health",           "Health").register()
val STAT_MEND         = StatEffect("stat_mend",             "Mend").register()
// …other stats…
val STATS = listOf(
    STAT_STRENGTH,
    STAT_ATTACK_SPEED,
    STAT_DAMAGE,
    STAT_HEALTH,
    STAT_MEND,
    // …etc…
)
```

At runtime, `StatEffectHandler.getValue(effect, player)` collects all active `stat_*` effects for the player, applies ADD/MULTIPLY_BASE/MULTIPLY operations in the proper sequence, and returns a `ModifiedStat` representing the final computed value. This ensures a consistent, extensible pipeline for all attribute‑based calculations.

#### Effect Providers

An **EffectProvider** is any pluggable data source that can supply `ActiveEffect` instances from arbitrary gameplay contexts—gear, consumables, skill trees, pets, temporary buffs, and more. Providers are registered on module startup via the central `PROVIDER_REGISTRY`:

```java
// ModuleEffects.java (onEnable)
AbstractEffectHandler.PROVIDER_REGISTRY.register("base_stats",     new BaseStatsProvider());
AbstractEffectHandler.PROVIDER_REGISTRY.register("items",          new MidnightItemEffectProvider());
AbstractEffectHandler.PROVIDER_REGISTRY.register("timed_effects",  TimedEffectProvider.INSTANCE);
AbstractEffectHandler.PROVIDER_REGISTRY.register("effect_package", PermissibleEffectHandler.INSTANCE);
```

Some providers include:

- **BaseStatsProvider**: supplies baseline stat effects (default Health, Damage, Mend, Attack Speed).
- **MidnightItemEffectProvider**: scans the player’s equipped armor and held items for custom `IPlayerEffectable`/`IEffectable` metadata and injects any Effects declared on those items.
- **TimedEffectProvider**: manages temporary, duration‑based effects (buffs, debuffs, cooldowns).
- **PermissibleEffectHandler**: hooks in permission‑based Effect packages for group or permission‑driven effect sets.

Additional providers are registered by other modules (adventure skills, island expansions, mob profiles, etc.), all leveraging the same registry. When resolving active effects for a player, the system queries every registered provider, merges their outputs, and caches the result for high‑performance lookups in both stat calculations and event‑driven triggers.

